iversity of  programs and courses that we can offer  our students  with this there are inherent challenges  trying to accommodate  busy work schedules trying to  accommodate those who are still  actively working in the field of  forensic science and  supplementing their lives with  additional teaching responsibilities  so what's really great about the  forensic science program is that we have  both  day and night courses and we can cater  to  at times those part-time students who  are trying to  fulfill that forensic science degree  dream  by offering a variety of coursework  our main tracks or disciplines that we  tend to focus on currently  are forensic anthropology forensic  biology  forensic chemistry and forensic  psychology so we offer  these four mainstreams currently and  we're  introducing in the coming years of  forensic toxicology stream  for those who are interested in pursuing  toxicology in their future  forensic anthropology and forensic  biology are both offered at the graduate  level  in terms of research-specific masters  and doctoral theses  however that is faculty dependent based  on the 10-year stream faculty who are  currently in the program  within our program at utm we have the  option for students to complete  a specialist degree so where their focus  is primarily in one of the main tracks  that i just mentioned  or a major degree where they're  completing  a forensic science major coupled with  another science or math major  or minors and then we also now offer a  minor program  so for those who have an interest in  forensic science but don't want to  pursue that as their main focus  at university there is a minor program  where they can supplement  their current learning or discipline  with some forensics classes just so that  they become familiar and are engaged in  the forensic sciences  just to give you a quick visual  insight into our program we do try and  provide our students  at all levels a very interactive and  engaging  experience this has a lot of hands-on  components so with that  there have been many inherent challenges  this year  of note or the things that i would like  to focus on the most are the fact that  we really pride ourselves on having  engaging lab experiences so all of our  disciplines  in the upper years have actual lab  components where  students are getting forensic biology  forensic chemistry forensic anthropology  hands-on experience  in the lab so that they understand  how these disciplines then translate  into the real world or how these  skills and techniques that they're  learning in lab  translate into the positions they may  hold in the future  and because our staff and faculty come  from backgrounds that are very rich  in terms of forensic experience we're  able to bring a very strong and  comprehensive program to our students we  also offer  field school and we tend to focus in two  specific areas so we have the forensic  anthropology field school  where students literally go out into the  woods  and are sifting through a crime scene  and recovering human remains and going  through all of the processes that are  necessary in order to  establish the the biological sex  ancestry etc of the human remains and  process that case in a traditional way  that a forensic anthropologist would  process it  we also have our forensic ident field  school and that is led by a retired  constable from the forensic ident unit  of a police agency in canada where  students then go through how a  traditional law enforcement officer  would process  scenes and so our students really are  getting a lot of hands-on  and field-based experience within our  undergraduate program  it's really wonderful that we're able to  offer such a  strong and diverse program to our  students  but you can understand that in light of  covid a lot  of our programming has been challenged  by the lack of  availability to have students on campus  as well as ensuring our social  distancing measures  and everything else that has come along  with the challenges that the pandemic  has brought  so i just want to quickly go through the  canadian covid19 timeline i know that  on a global scale we're all well aware  of what's happened  but thinking more regionally for us and  how our program specifically has  been impacted i think it's just  important or  might be helpful for everyone who's  watching to understand what we did and  how we were able to respond  so thinking back to last year march 16th  obviously was a big turning point we had  our official closure  of the university all three campuses  all courses and all research labs were  immediately halted and switched  to online pro processes  what this meant was we were at the tail  end of our semester our semester ends in  early april  so we did encounter several obstacles  trying to immediately transition to an  online  environment but u of t put a halt  on everything for that two-week initial  window while we  navigated what the pandemic was going to  bring  of course as everyone is well aware  those  those closures were well extended past  the two-week period  so everything in the winter term for  2020 was then pushed online  including a final exam session  which was obviously a first for the  university  and faculty and staff really stepped up  to navigate what that would look like  and of course there were a lot of  challenges we encountered  increased amounts of plagiarism  we encountered increased amounts of  technical difficulties  time zone issues everything that you can  surely imagine  or experience yourself in terms of that  initial  reaction to the immediate closure  in may we launched our first remote only  term  for summer coursework all of the  research labs  and in-field or  practical components of our coursework  remained closed or  not offered so that was an incredibly  unique challenge  especially from the forensic science  program where we had students already  enrolled in those field schools  and we're gearing up for graduation  in june the university issued an  opportunity to  reopen labs with very very strict  criteria  so all labs were assessed in terms of  space and social distancing capabilities  how many individuals can we safely put  in our labs  what ppe is required to ensure that our  labs are operating safely  etc we were required to submit  comprehensive lab reopening plans in  terms of  who is required to be on campus so who  are our designated personnel  what students really needed to be on  campus  again trying to mitigate the risk for  everybody  and this was approved through our office  of the vice principal research  we also had very strict hours in terms  of our lab  operations so 7 am to 4 pm  to ensure that our facilities and  anybody that needed to be able to  respond in case of emergency was  available so no more of those late  nights in the lab  everything was quite restricted to those  daytime hours  so over this summer we saw a lot of lab  reopenings we saw a little bit of  movement back to campus from faculty in  terms of  maybe just wanting to get back in the  office to settle affairs that had been  lingering from  the spring term and then trying to  really  gear up and prep for the fall and  whatever that was going to look like  our fall term brought a hybrid learning  environment  meaning that the university did offer  critical courses that were required in  person hybrid courses where students had  the option to either  be at home or come into the classroom  and then those courses that were offered  completely online  why we had such a diversity of course  offerings was to cater to our students  so the university of toronto in general  brings in quite a few international  students we have a large international  student  body and with the restrictions for  travel and bringing students back with  the canadian government policies  i.e that 14-day mandatory quarantine it  was very difficult  to have students return and then be  forced to stay in their homes  and then be under the stay-at-home order  policies that exist in ontario  currently and then back in the fall so  we were trying to essentially create an  environment  that was conducive to all essential  coursework like i mentioned was allowed  to go forward in person if necessary  so those critical labs where students  required getting those hands-on skills  where something couldn't be provided in  a remote setting  moved ahead in person what this meant  was  a the faculty member had to be  comfortable b  the teaching assistants had to be  comfortable to come on campus  and see the students enrolled in the  course had to be comfortable to come on  campus  so of course you can understand that  this brought in and of itself  quite a few challenges in october  within the province of ontario where  toronto  is we had increased lockdown measures  and these varied but continued to cycle  through the end of the year  this made it increasingly challenging  and stressful for faculty and students  alike as we were constantly navigating  what policies were in place at the  government level  and then at the university level in  order to navigate our instruction  and our ability to complete the courses  moving into 2021 we again kept a hybrid  learning  environment offered through our winter  term  winter in canada obviously is quite cold  we have the increased challenges of  weather etc  and so here again faculty and students  alike were navigating  what was critical when did we have to be  on campus  so faculty in this instance moved more  towards the hybrid learning when  possible  and really reduced the number of  critical or essential courses that were  taught  on campus the government restrictions  have  increased in 2021 making it even more  difficult  to conduct experiments and be on campus  so in in that context it was very  helpful to have an increased number of  hybrid or  completely remote part of me completely  remote course  offerings for our students april again  brought a new stay-at-home order as our  cases continue to increase  and this again further  restricted our ability to conduct  experiments on campus  and to provide course offerings such as  the field schools to our students  going into the summer of 2021 so where  we are now  remote learning is currently dominating  due to the increased number of variant  cases  in the greater toronto area  okay so thinking back to spring and  summer 2020  i really want to focus on the pivot at u  of t  so thinking about forensic science  education and thinking about  all of the course offerings that we have  for our students we really had to  transition to this remote learning  environment  in order to serve the diversity of  students that we had  remembering that u of t has a very large  international student body we had a lot  of our students returning back to their  home provinces and home countries  and it was very important to us to  ensure that they were not derailed  from their academic trajectory and so  in light of that we ensured that we  could  offer the courses that they required but  in a remote setting  for example our forensic ethics course  using our very innovative and skilled  staff and faculty we were able to  develop this  train ride or roller coaster of ethics  where the student would essentially go  down the tracks and make ethical  decisions  that would lead them either further  along the path or  to a point of destruction and it allowed  them to essentially  test their ethical barometer per se  in a way that was engaging and that  integrated the course material  in a very um intellectually stimulating  way and so as we continue to move  forward in this online delivery we're  developing more and more tools  such as this ethical roller coaster to  challenge our students to think about  the course material  while still being engaged at the  computer  of course when it came to labs we didn't  have the ability to offer those  in-person placements so we moved to  online demos  and we cancelled our in-person  opportunities  over the summer what the online demos  typically entailed was  a youtube or a first person view of the  experiment so that students could  essentially visualize what they would be  doing with their hands  without actually carrying it out  themselves of course this brings  large challenges within the field of  forensic  science simply because you need to be  able to have those technical skills to  then walk  into a crime lab or to them walk into  another lab setting  so a little bit of challenge there in  terms of  really developing the skills for the  students however  we are doing the best we can to ensure  that they are getting  that experience or they're getting the  theory and  at best possible practical component of  the course  we of course had to abort or cancel a  lot of the ongoing projects  so thinking back to march 2020 a lot of  students were working on their capstone  projects  and those came to an abrupt end in terms  of the field  or in-person components what that meant  was  we wrapped up what we could and we made  do with the results that were already  tabulated  however those projects that were  potentially going to publication were  then put on hold until experiments could  be completed  that was of course a big disappointment  for both faculty  practitioners and students alike but i'm  hopeful  that we are going to get back on track  this year  the second point i want to bring up was  the forensic science research  collaborations and funding  so as i'm sure most people are aware we  had a big shift in terms  of funding allocation where most  funding was reallocated to covet 19  scientific research and that is  completely acceptable and understandable  in light of the severity of the pandemic  however that does  obviously impose severe challenges on  those not working in that area  additionally there were some constraints  on the collaborations with outside  agencies and labs  simply because everybody was navigating  and pivoting in their own direction  and maintaining some of those strong  collaborative  projects were were stressed or put on  hold a little bit  in terms of re-navigating what type of  in-person  exchange of either reagents or samples  or sample collection etc could be  conducted until things  improved  thinking to fall 2020 our new normal  we have navigated into this hybrid  system  of having a lot of remote only options  having some hybrid teachings and then  only those essential  in-person lab offerings are very  specific  in person necessary course requirements  the university of toronto continues to  maintain  this strong availability of remote  learning  that way it is accessible to our  international students  so we don't want to turn away our  international students in light of the  pandemic we want to be able to  assist them through this as much as is  possible  so then when the time is right we can  bring them back on campus  the hybrid model also works well for  international students because it allows  that flexibility for those who are local  to come on campus  but those who are far away to still  experience  and participate in the learning outcomes  of the courses  without having to be physically present  the only limitation  and this is specific to the forensic  science program  is the in-person component so those  courses like forensic biology or  forensic  chemistry that have the critical lab  components  or in forensic anthropology or forensic  ident  with the field schools international  students will not be able necessarily to  take those courses  or large amendments or accommodations  will be required  in order to ensure that our  international students can still  progress through our program  without being penalized simply because  they can't be on campus  so we are still navigating how that will  work  but of course that is a big challenge we  ultimately want our students who are  going through this program to develop  the technical and practical skills  necessary  to then go on into their future careers  but of course as the cases continue to  drop and as the restrictions around the  globe continue to  change and at times the restrictions  lesson  we are bringing more and more  international students back to campus  in as safe as is possible manner to get  them  back into the lab or to get them back  into the field school so they can wrap  up their programs  and move on  so again bringing us back to where we  are now  like i mentioned there is a lot of  fluctuation in terms  of government regulation and policy we  are  currently in a stay-at-home order within  the province of  ontario meaning that absolutely  nothing is proceeding on campus that is  not absolutely critical  and this involves lab operations as well  so that is creating a little bit more of  a restrictive  environment for our students but we have  been able to take this past year to  really navigate how to perfect or  improve our online delivery for everyone  involved  it also means a big shift in terms of  our forensic scientific projects  so both at the undergraduate and  graduate levels we have a change in  terms of how our projects are designed  so instead of doing sample collection  from individuals  we're approaching our projects in terms  of data mining or looking at previously  generated data  or repurposing some of our forensic data  that has been  applied to other projects into new  directions  so this is allowing us to alleviate some  of that tension  in terms of not having enough projects  for students or  feeling like we can't provide them with  an invaluable experience  but it does change the approach  or understanding of forensic concepts  when students aren't getting that in  person or in lab  experience  thinking quickly about the impacts on  both  undergraduates and graduate students  from the undergraduate perspective  the observed impacts i think are  multi-fold so we're seeing a change in  resilience  students are more flexible it seems with  last minute changes  but we are also seeing an increased  number of mental health  issues as a result of potential  isolation  and the various challenges of doing or  participating in online learning from  home  not everybody has the perfect setup not  everybody has the privacy  not everybody can commit the focus  necessary to truly engage in some of the  coursework that's being delivered  remotely  so of course we are seeing variable  levels of resilience  from our undergraduates we're also  seeing a shift in expectations  so for some students in vulnerable  households there is an expectation to  accommodate so that they do not have to  come to campus  and this is understandable but within  the forensic science program  it becomes challenging when we do  require an in-person component for some  of our courses  because not having them in the classroom  means that they will not develop those  practical skills that are required for  the field  but we also want to be respectful and  accommodate whatever it is  that they are for the challenges that  they're  encountering within the pandemic there's  also a demand or an increased  pressure for virtual over in-person  learning and again we're doing our best  to navigate what  is best for the program and best for our  students while still ensuring that they  are receiving those learning outcomes  are able to develop  the skills necessary from the courses  that we're offering  of course the frustration is real for  everybody  the constant flux in policies and  procedures both at the institutional and  government levels  are creating unnecessary stress for some  of our students  in terms of are we going to be in person  are we going to be remote  etc so we are seeing some variability  there  in terms of the overall mood in the  classroom  the lack of experience i think is the  most important  aspect to consider from the  undergraduate impact perspective  simply because if you're not getting the  technical skills how are you then  competitive to go into the job market  and that is something that of course  we're doing our best  to mitigate and we're doing our best to  accommodate in terms of developing  skills and providing invaluable research  opportunities  when the time and the restrictions  permit  and then of course going back to those  abandoned projects and publications  that brings in the frustration and the  shifted expectations  but we do have several projects that are  still incomplete  but then present the new opportunity  that when things restore or reopen  we'll have multiple people engaged in  these projects to wrap them up and get  quick publications out  the graduate impact is slightly  different obviously graduate students  have very tailored or specifically  focused projects  but we're seeing a big shift in  responsibility so in the past year we've  obviously  increased our demand for teaching  assistance or  online course offerings and that has  brought a need for the graduate students  to shift from their research into these  teaching roles  that's great in terms of developing that  teaching and leadership experience but  that does create an  impact on their overall research  timeline  and their focus as a graduate student  throughout their program  the shift in focus in terms of projects  has also changed so looking at more data  analysis  or remote opportunities rather than  in-person lab or sample collection based  projects  and again depending on the discipline  this can be  more challenging in some fields than  others  and the university restrictions in terms  of research activities  with regard to travel so if there are  collaborations with  agencies outside of the province or  outside of the country  there are quite severe research  restrictions currently in place  that prevent students from going to  collect samples or perform their  necessary venture field work  in order to collect that data so we are  seeing some challenges there  that have really been exacerbated over  the past year  finally we have this concept of delayed  timelines  and funding challenges so obviously  timelines are being skewed  with the shift to teaching and  responsibility  and then delays in their overall  research and then the funding associated  with that  so reallocation of funds again to covet  research and projects has created some  disparity in some disciplines especially  the forensic sciences in canada  and we also have this concept of loss of  grant support so students that are  currently funded  in light of the shifts away from  research  it does create these weird bottlenecks  in terms of how their funding will look  in the coming years if they have to  extend their timelines  so that is where we are right now we  have these funding challenges  in terms of how we're coordinating with  agencies how we're coordinating with our  grant funding  etc and what the long-term impacts of  those will be  are still quite unknown we have our  staffing challenges so we have  reduced or limited capacity staffing on  campus and in the lab  but we also have to increase our staff  for our virtual  and remote delivery safety is  always the number one concern at the  university of toronto  so we are being very respectful of the  space and time restrictions that are in  place both in the classroom and in the  lab  and there's currently no expectation for  any students  especially graduate students to be on  campus unless  critical experiments or operations are  required  and then finally as we can all imagine  there are impacts in terms of  productivity  we have this reallocation to a growing  number of emails and our inboxes  and a growing number of zoom meetings in  our calendars  that are taking away from our ability to  conduct research  further the pressure of constantly being  at the computer can be quite draining  and so we are seeing burnout quite a bit  sooner than we would typically expect if  students and faculty were at the bench  and that takes us to our next steps so  thinking about everything that i've  discussed in terms of the university of  toronto lens  when we think about forensic science  research and education  what does our future new normal look  like are we going to be making a lot of  adjusted timelines for completion and  are we going to be restructuring our  research focus  for graduate student projects or  undergraduate capstone projects etc  are we going to be moving towards a data  analysis  approach where you have very few people  in the lab generating as much data as  possible  that can then be disseminated outward or  how do we continue to move the field  forward  if we're really limiting our in-person  experiences  second are international students and  collaborators  how do we ensure that we can maintain  these relationships how are we still  satisfying our international students  when we have  strict restrictions in terms of bringing  our international students back  thinking simply about the united states  canada border  we have a lot of restrictions there in  terms  of flight travel in terms of land  crossings  and that can be very impeding in terms  of fostering relationships with our  collaborators and ensuring that we can  go and acquire our samples or be present  in each other's  labs to develop our research endeavors  and then finally just overall  collaborative nature  with outside agencies everybody is  adjusting to new policies and procedures  and ensuring that we  can still foster a very  conducive environment for research  collaboration  is going to be critical and so  navigating those next steps is going to  be interesting  but it seems that everybody wants to get  back to work  or a lot of people want to get back to  work and we're seeing a growing interest  in redeveloping those relationships  between  both the academic unit and our outside  collaborating agencies  obviously networking and collaborative  challenges from  the zoom perspective so wanting to get  back to those in-person conferences  there's a lot of burnout in terms of  virtual conference delivery  the doom of zoom i like to say um  we're navigating what that looks like  but of course we're seeing an  increase in hybrid delivery at least for  2021 but 2022 and 2023 conferences  seem to be moving to in-person  opportunities so that does bring  some optimism for that networking and  collaborative  future and then finally this concept of  establishing the virtual lab  so like i mentioned we have been using  tools like  gopro um and having that headset from  the first person view to  show students what it looks like to be  performing these experiments that  they're not getting  to do themselves and i like to think of  it more like the bill nye the science  guy  type of learning environment where  students can follow along the  experiments  and then apply the theoretical knowledge  that they've acquired in their courses  to really pull all of the concepts  together to ensure  that they've met all of the learning  objectives in the course  so then i want to turn it to you the  audience and really wonder  what are the institutional differences  that you see or that you've observed  between  your organization or institution and  what's happening here at the university  of toronto  as i can imagine on a global scale  there's quite a bit of variability  just in terms of institutional  regulations  and either provincial state or  at the national level regulations and  policies  as it relates to covid19 so what does  your teaching or learning  experience look like how has your  institution  pivoted or established a new normal for  forensic science education and research  primarily at the undergraduate level but  i would also love to hear what's  happening at the graduate level  and how student projects have pivoted in  that direction  how have your collaborations or  experiential learning opportunities been  restored  since all of these challenges have been  in place and now where are you going  from here  because i think that this is a great  opportunity as  the field of forensic science given that  we are so tight  knit to really share in our challenges  and obstacles and then  things that are working well now in  terms of just being able to help each  other out  and move forward in as positive and as  collaborative ways as possible so please  send me your feedback i would really  love to hear  what is working for you or what didn't  work for you  and what you're doing now as a  consequence of that  so thank you very much for listening i  really hope that you were able to take  something away from today's presentation  i would very much love to hear from you  so here is my contact information  my lab website if you're interested in  learning more specifically about what i  do  within the field of forensic genetics  and  again thank you for being here and have  a wonderful day

